    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263-a1">

                <pb facs="0371" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg253"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Annex]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Chief of the Division of
                        Far Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-07-19">July 19, 1929</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Illustrative Passages Dealing With
                        the Question of the Right of Self-Defense Found in Correspondence Between
                        Certain Powers Signatory to the General Pact for the Renunciation of
                        War</hi></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The French Ambassador to the Secretary of State, March 30, 1928, page 17,
                        the paragraph beginning “My Government likewise gathers”.<anchor n="22"
                            xml:id="d263-f1"/></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The German Minister of Foreign Affairs to the American Ambassador, April
                        27, 1928, page 24, the paragraph beginning “The German Government
                            proceeds”.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d263-f2"/></item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs to the American
                        Ambassador, May 19, 1928, page 26, paragraph 4.<anchor n="24"
                            xml:id="d263-f3"/></item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>The Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs to the American Ambassador, May
                        26, 1928, page 31, the paragraph beginning “The proposal of the United
                        States is understood”.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d263-f4"/></item>
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>The British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs to the American Chargé
                        d’Affaires, June 15, 1928, page 35, the paragraph beginning “In expressing
                        their willingness to be a party to the proposed treaty, His Majesty’s
                        Government in the Union of South Africa took it for granted”.<anchor n="26"
                            xml:id="d263-f5"/></item>
                    <pb facs="0372" n="254" xml:id="pg254"/>
                    <label>6.</label>
                    <item>Note of the Government of the United States to certain other governments,
                        June 23, 1928, page 36, paragraph beginning “(1) Self-Defense.”<anchor
                            n="27" xml:id="d263-f6"/> In the same document, page 38, the second
                        paragraph.</item>
                    <label>7.</label>
                    <item>The Polish Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs to the American Minister,
                        July 8 [<hi rendition="#italic">17?</hi>] 1928, page 42, the last
                            paragraph.<anchor n="28" xml:id="d263-f7"/></item>
                    <label>8.</label>
                    <item>The French Minister of Foreign Affairs to the American Ambassador, dated
                        July 14, 1928, page 44, the paragraph beginning “Nothing in the new treaty
                            restrains”.<anchor n="29" xml:id="d263-f8"/></item>
                    <label>9.</label>
                    <item>The Italian Minister of Foreign Affairs to the American Ambassador, July
                        15, 1928, page 46, the second paragraph.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d263-f9"
                        /></item>
                    <label>10.</label>
                    <item>The Belgian Minister of Foreign Affairs to the American Ambassador, July
                        17, 1928, page 46, the paragraph beginning “The text prepared”.<anchor
                            n="31" xml:id="d263-f10"/></item>
                    <label>11.</label>
                    <item>The British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs to the American Chargé
                        d’Affaires, page 48, the paragraph beginning, “I am entirely in
                            accord”.<anchor n="32" xml:id="d263-f11"/></item>
                    <label>12.</label>
                    <item>The British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, on behalf of the
                        Commonwealth of Australia, to the American Chargé d’Affaires, July 18, 1928,
                        page 49, paragraph 2.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d263-f12"/></item>
                    <label>13.</label>
                    <item>The Czechoslovak Minister of Foreign Affairs to the American Minister,
                        July 20, 1928, page 52, paragraph 3.<anchor n="34" xml:id="d263-f13"
                        /></item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
